 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S444-005\", \"clauses\": {\"solve_problems\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F10\", \"template\": \"How many {names} are left?\", \"reasoning\": \"F10 prints a question asking how many remain after a take-away, which is a problem the learner solves. F10 is itself a failing family (see overall reasoning), but that does not stop it producing the task.\"}, \"in_pictures\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F16\", \"template\": \"{p0}{p1}{p2}{p3}{p4}{p5}{p6}{p7}\", \"reasoning\": \"F16 prints an 8-item emoji strip as the group that F13 nests, so a problem can be given in pictures. The picture count is not tied to a in F13, which is a failure of F13, not of this clause.\"}, \"involving_subtraction\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F12\", \"template\": \"Taking away {b} {name}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F12 places the problem in an explicit take-away (subtraction) context.\"}, \"less_than_100\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F13\", \"template\": \"{group} — There {verb} {a} {name}.\", \"reasoning\": \"The a slot in F13 has observed runs from 2 to 93 and the declared minuend bound is 1 to 99; the b slot in F12 has observed runs up to 80. Both numbers can be under 100.\"}, \"regrouping\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"The ones digit {oa} is less than {ob}, so we need to {reg}. Regroup 1 ten as 10 ones: {oa10} − {ob} = {od}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 prints the regrouping step for a problem whose ones digit must be regrouped. The without-regrouping case is produced by the ones-step family F03 and the tens-step family F04, and the declared regrouping variants none and one_place cover both cases.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F03\", \"F04\", \"F05\", \"F08\", \"F09\", \"F10\", \"F11\", \"F12\", \"F13\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Subtract the ones: {oa} − {ob} = {od}.\", \"Subtract the tens: {ta} − {tb} = {td}.{note}\", \"The ones digit {oa} is less than {ob}, so we need to {reg}. Regroup 1 ten as 10 ones: {oa10} − {ob} = {od}.\", \"(Large group of {a} {pluralize})\", \"(Large group of {remaining} {pluralize})\", \"How many {names} are left?\", \"Taking away 0 {names}.\", \"Taking away {b} {name}.\", \"{group} — There {verb} {a} {name}.\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"Rule 1 fails on several independent domain defects. (1) F05 states that the ones digit is less than the subtrahend ones digit, but oa and ob are generator-computed from a and b with no declared constraint that oa is less than ob, and template_chosen_by_values is empty, so the false statement can print in problems that need no regrouping. (2) F04 has a note slot whose members include a regrouping remark, and the note has no derived link to any value and no template choice, so a regrouping remark can print on a tens step that involved no regrouping. (3) F03 prints a ones-step difference od whose domain is the image of subtracting ones digits and has no lower bound declared, so it can be negative when ob exceeds oa. (4) F08 and F09 pair a count of 21 or more, or a remaining count, with a noun that may be singular, so a singular noun with a plural count can print. (5) F10 has no count slot at all and its noun may be singular, so a singular noun with a plural question can print. (6) F11 prints zero with a noun that may be singular, which is ungrammatical. (7) F12 prints b with a noun that may be singular, and b has observed value 1, so a singular noun with a plural count can print. (8) F13 chooses its verb from is or are and its noun from the list or its plural with no link to a, so a singular verb or noun with a count of 2 or more can print. F13 also pairs its count a with a picture group whose emoji count is fixed by the template length (2, 8, 10, 12, 15, 16, 17, 18 or 20) and not derived from a, so the picture can disagree with the count. The emoji families F15 to F23 are not listed because their own text is an emoji strip; the fault lies in F13's pairing. The plural spellings the pluralize domain produces are not printed in the packet and are not judged. Vocabulary, sentence length (all templates under 18 words), number format and the less-than-100 bound are not at fault.\"}",
